Introduction to Applied Linear Algebra — Boyd and Vandenberghe (@pe-linear-algebra: 0, is #reference, #part-of @pe-sources) { r_3684D74C735KQS61ST3EH0XKZW
Stephen Boyd and Lieven Vandenberghe. Introduction to Applied Linear Algebra: Vectors, Matrices, and Least Squares. Cambridge University Press. [Author's complete book and course resources](https://web.stanford.edu/~boyd/vmls/).

Read the whole book when developing the matrix foundation for power-flow linearization, networks, parameter estimation and optimization. Work vector/matrix, least-squares, rank, conditioning and constrained-approximation examples, then connect them to actual bus, measurement and parameter meanings. Reconstruct calculations with a small independent problem before using a large package.

The author page supplies a complete authorized downloadable book and links its Stanford/UCLA courses; checked on 29 October 2026. Publisher copyright and the author's permission to host a free copy do not automatically create a CC licence. Companion code and exercise/solution resources have their own notices and availability. Nothing is bundled.

**Return:** [Power-engineering sources — complete books, courses, methods and evidence](../Sources.lingua#r_12EFX00X7ZXGT9MPMJDYRTNY9N).

**Second mathematics-block inspection — 31 October 2026:** the author page and authorized complete PDF were checked. The contents and selected passages on vector operations, norms and distance, dependence/bases, matrix action, linear/affine maps, composition and inverses were inspected, including the opening explanations of sections 5.1–5.2, 8.1, 10.2 and 11.2. The common block assigns sections 1.1–1.4, 3.1–3.2, 3.4, 5.1–5.3, 6.1, 6.4, 7.1, 8.1, 8.3, 10.1–10.2 and 11.1–11.2 progressively, with preparation and exercises. This broader assignment is not a claim that every passage and exercise was audited. Taylor approximation, Gram–Schmidt, QR, least squares and general numerical linear algebra retain later preparation. The original power-engineering whole-book assignment remains intact. The temporary PDF inspection was outside the repository; source media remain external and the publisher copyright applies.


**Fifth mathematics-core inspection — 31 October 2026:** the official author page was freshly checked for identity, authorized access and publisher copyright; PDF title/contents retrieval succeeded, but subsequent web PDF requests timed out. Selected passages were therefore inspected in the previously downloaded authorized copy used for the earlier mathematics block: section 5.4 on Gram–Schmidt and its orthogonality/span argument, section 10.4 on orthonormal columns and QR context, and sections 12.2–12.3 on the direct squared-norm proof, residual orthogonality and QR solution. Printed section starts are 97, 189, 227 and 231. The new common block assigns 5.4, 10.4 and 12.1–12.3 after the earlier vector/matrix preparation. Complete sections, source examples/exercises and numerical stability analysis were not audited or performed. The original skip-and-continue orthogonalization and rank-deficient investigation explicitly develop their own domain and proofs; they do not inherit a full-column-rank inverse formula. Earlier inspections and whole-book power-engineering assignments remain. Author-hosted availability does not grant a CC license; no source PDF, figure or code is bundled.

